patients and wipes out symptoms in 60%; it is not the electrocution of One Flew Over the Cuckoo&#x27;s Nest but the oldest somatic treatment to survive the slaughterhouse, the asylum, and the anti-psychiatry movement.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Psychiatry</span><span class=\"tag\">ECT</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"A State Court Authorized the Security Test. The Sheriff Said the Contract Was Void. A state court&#x27;s authorization letter carries no weight over a county courthouse building, so a contracted security test becomes burglary. Even after every charge is dropped, the arrest record stays on the books forever — the justice system can pay money, but it cannot restore a clean record. Darknet Diaries Darknet Diaries 179: The Courthouse - Revisited Penetration Testing Physical Security Wrongful Arrest Legal Accountability Immunity Startups qualified immunity sovereign immunity\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-09-01-darknetdiaries-持州法院授权测安防-警长却说合同无效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/efb1e077bc2ff15f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">1:35:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Darknet Diaries</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0901</span></div>\n<h2>A State Court Authorized the Security Test. The Sheriff Said the Contract Was Void.</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A state court&#x27;s authorization letter carries no weight over a county courthouse building, so a contracted security test becomes burglary. Even after every charge is dropped, the arrest record stays on the books forever — the justice system can pay money, but it cannot restore a clean record.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Penetration Testing</span><span class=\"tag\">Physical Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Residual Connections: Deep Learning&#x27;s Most Underrated Simple Hack ResNet is the most cited paper of the 21st century, but its real contribution isn&#x27;t making networks deeper — it&#x27;s inventing the residual stream, a kind of working memory that lets a model store and edit information, and that fundamentally changed how we understand neural networks. WelchLabsVideo WelchLabsVideo The most cited paper of the century is a brilliant hack ResNet Deep Learning Transformer Working Memory Vision Models Architecture Residual stream Skip connection Register tokens Shattered gradients problem\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-09-01-welchlabsvideo-残差连接-深度学习史上最被低估的简单-hack/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a540a5f22c57ac52-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">WelchLabsVideo</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0901</span></div>\n<h2>Residual Connections: Deep Learning&#x27;s Most Underrated Simple Hack</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">ResNet is the most cited paper of the 21st century, but its real contribution isn&#x27;t making networks deeper — it&#x27;s inventing the residual stream, a kind of working memory that lets a model store and edit information, and that fundamentally changed how we understand neural networks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">ResNet</span><span class=\"tag\">Deep Learning</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The Fed&#x27;s Credibility Breaks, and the Options Market Shrugs The Fed&#x27;s credibility cracked over a single decision not to hike, while the options market still prices Fed day like any other day — the cheapest insurance may be an illusion. Excess Returns Excess Returns Sticky Inflation. Cheap Volatility. A Less Predictable Fed. Why Aren’t Markets More Worried? Fed Volatility Options Strategy Inflation Midterms AI Writing forward volatility demand-based inflation zero DTE straddle jawboning financial repression calendarized gamma\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-excessreturns-美联储信誉崩塌-期权市场却当无事发生/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3cac5e72b6049e76-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Excess Returns</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>The Fed&#x27;s Credibility Breaks, and the Options Market Shrugs</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Fed&#x27;s credibility cracked over a single decision not to hike, while the options market still prices Fed day like any other day — the cheapest insurance may be an illusion.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Fed</span><span class=\"tag\">Volatility</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"China&#x27;s Economic Peak Is Behind It: Credit Stalls, Deflation Looms China&#x27;s share of global GDP peaked in 2021 and has fallen since; credit growth has dropped from 18% to 5%, real growth may be just 1.5%-2%, and the US has now outperformed for five straight years. The supply-chain-dominance narrative is falsified by the data; the future looks more like Japanese-style deflation. Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch The Miracle Is Over: Inside China&#x27;s Slow-Motion Economic Decay China economy credit PPP demographics US-China competition trade protection PPP / Purchasing Power Parity Impossible Trinity Loan Prime Rate (LPR) Special Revenue Bonds Total Factor Productivity (TFP) Demand-side Controls\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-geopoliticsdec-中国经济巅峰已过-美国优势还将扩大/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8de827bd7254104a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">47:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Geopolitics Decanted with Dmitri Alperovitch</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>China&#x27;s Economic Peak Is Behind It: Credit Stalls, Deflation Looms</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">China&#x27;s share of global GDP peaked in 2021 and has fallen since; credit growth has dropped from 18% to 5%, real growth may be just 1.5%-2%, and the US has now outperformed for five straight years. The supply-chain-dominance narrative is falsified by the data; the future looks more like Japanese-style deflation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">China economy</span><span class=\"tag\">credit</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Bell Used Einstein&#x27;s Own Tools to Refute His Central Claim The EPR argument forces you to choose between &quot;the quantum description is incomplete&quot; and &quot;action at a distance&quot;; determinism was never a premise but an inferred conclusion. The full logic of Bell&#x27;s theorem has to start here. Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal Tim Maudlin: Quantum Nonlocality Explained FROM SCRATCH Quantum mechanics EPR Bell&#x27;s theorem Nonlocality History of physics Action at a distance EPR / Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen action at a distance configuration space reality criterion ontological locality dynamical locality\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-theoriesofever-贝尔用爱因斯坦自己的工具推翻了他的核心论点/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/06123fc0486c117a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">3:18:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Theories of Everything with Curt Jaimungal</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Bell Used Einstein&#x27;s Own Tools to Refute His Central Claim</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The EPR argument forces you to choose between &quot;the quantum description is incomplete&quot; and &quot;action at a distance&quot;; determinism was never a premise but an inferred conclusion. The full logic of Bell&#x27;s theorem has to start here.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Quantum mechanics</span><span class=\"tag\">EPR</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"The quantum endgame isn&#x27;t whose qubits are cleaner, it&#x27;s who can mass-produce them on a phone-chip line The endgame of quantum computing isn&#x27;t whose qubits are cleaner, it&#x27;s who can mass-produce them on existing semiconductor lines; silicon spin qubits reuse TSMC&#x27;s lithography, while trapped ions are stuck at a hundred thousand laser beams and superconductors at warehouse-scale refrigerators. From First Principles From First Principles Why Spin Qubits Will Win the Quantum Race (Part 2) (EP 55) Quantum computing Silicon spin Semiconductors Superconducting Trapped ions AI auto-tuning transmon valley splitting 2DEG DETR exchange-only qubit\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-fromfirstprinc-硅自旋将赢-百万比特只需一块硅晶圆/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3248f02d6e6be9fe-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">3:46:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From First Principles</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>The quantum endgame isn&#x27;t whose qubits are cleaner, it&#x27;s who can mass-produce them on a phone-chip line</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The endgame of quantum computing isn&#x27;t whose qubits are cleaner, it&#x27;s who can mass-produce them on existing semiconductor lines; silicon spin qubits reuse TSMC&#x27;s lithography, while trapped ions are stuck at a hundred thousand laser beams and superconductors at warehouse-scale refrigerators.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Quantum computing</span><span class=\"tag\">Silicon spin</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI chip shipments are about to jump an order of magnitude: from a million units to 15 million Broadcom&#x27;s president lays out the AI chip roadmap: from a million units to 15 million, with the &quot;beast&quot; being the largest package at 8 stackable compute dies plus 16 HBM, and four of the five frontier labs working with the company. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato Future GPUs Will Look Like This Custom silicon ASIC Advanced packaging HBM Broadcom Compute supply chain back-end design services HBM CoWoS scaleup fabric\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-techtechpotato-未来-ai-定制芯片的形态-一只带-16-个-hbm-的巨兽/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7f1cfd222dffd8f1-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">11:10</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>AI chip shipments are about to jump an order of magnitude: from a million units to 15 million</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Broadcom&#x27;s president lays out the AI chip roadmap: from a million units to 15 million, with the &quot;beast&quot; being the largest package at 8 stackable compute dies plus 16 HBM, and four of the five frontier labs working with the company.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Custom silicon</span><span class=\"tag\">ASIC</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"An Explorer Died for a Phantom City, and the Trail Starts with a Political Forgery Percy Fawcett&#x27;s Amazon expeditions are usually told as adventure legend, but the real starting point was a border dispute driven by the rubber trade; his &quot;respect&quot; for Indigenous peoples is badly oversimplified, and the City of Z that consumed him may rest on a forged manuscript. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Percy Fawcett, Manuscript 512, and a Lost City in the Amazon, Pt. 1 Exploration history Amazon Rubber trade Indigenous peoples Mysticism Lost city Manuscript 512 Royal Geographical Society rubber boom Mato Grosso City of Z\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-stuffmissed-福西特不是去冒险-是去给橡胶资本画地图/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">34:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>An Explorer Died for a Phantom City, and the Trail Starts with a Political Forgery</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Percy Fawcett&#x27;s Amazon expeditions are usually told as adventure legend, but the real starting point was a border dispute driven by the rubber trade; his &quot;respect&quot; for Indigenous peoples is badly oversimplified, and the City of Z that consumed him may rest on a forged manuscript.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Exploration history</span><span class=\"tag\">Amazon</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Time Is Not an Illusion: Irreversibility Is More Fundamental Than Symmetric Equations Jim Al-Khalili argues that time is real and directed, and that irreversibility is more fundamental than time-symmetric equations; he also proposes replacing thermodynamic entropy with quantum entanglement entropy as the basis for the past hypothesis. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 366 | Jim Al-Khalili on Time, Quantum, Biology, and Cosmology Arrow of Time Quantum Mechanics Quantum Biology Entropy Past Hypothesis Philosophy of Physics past hypothesis entanglement entropy master equation decoherence non-Markovian many-worlds interpretation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-mindscape-时间的方向性比时间对称方程更根本/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d69097efcbd03611-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:15:16</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Time Is Not an Illusion: Irreversibility Is More Fundamental Than Symmetric Equations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Jim Al-Khalili argues that time is real and directed, and that irreversibility is more fundamental than time-symmetric equations; he also proposes replacing thermodynamic entropy with quantum entanglement entropy as the basis for the past hypothesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Arrow of Time</span><span class=\"tag\">Quantum Mechanics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Most Gazans Have Already Abandoned Hamas, and a Disarmament Window Is Opening Gaza-born policy scholar Alkhatib argues from four layers of opposition, two polls and demographic data that most Gazans turned against Hamas long ago and that Hamas may be forced into phased disarmament — but host Russ&#x27;s pessimism is a sharp counterpoint: for Hamas, disarmament equals political suicide. EconTalk EconTalk Hamas and the Future of Gaza (with Ahmed Fouad Alkhatib) Gaza Hamas Israel-Palestine Poll Manipulation Demilitarization Middle East Geopolitics Shikaki polls NCAG Great Reset UNRWA DSA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-econtalk-加沙多数人已抛弃哈马斯-缴械窗口正在打开/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:22:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Most Gazans Have Already Abandoned Hamas, and a Disarmament Window Is Opening</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Gaza-born policy scholar Alkhatib argues from four layers of opposition, two polls and demographic data that most Gazans turned against Hamas long ago and that Hamas may be forced into phased disarmament — but host Russ&#x27;s pessimism is a sharp counterpoint: for Hamas, disarmament equals political suicide.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Gaza</span><span class=\"tag\">Hamas</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"US Economic Resilience Comes From Consumers Borrowing From the Future Barkin breaks down why spending stays strong despite high prices: consumers cut savings, fall behind on bills, and defer loan payments to keep spending; AI investment so far is more about price increases and crowding out than a productivity dividend; the Fed may face a long headwind. Odd Lots Odd Lots Richmond Fed’s Tom Barkin on the Surprisingly Resilient Real Economy Consumer resilience Federal Reserve AI capex Inflation Data centers Tariffs SEP / Summary of Economic Projections dot plot R-star / neutral rate forward guidance private label\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-oddlots-美国经济韧性来自消费者向未来借钱/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">31:17</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>US Economic Resilience Comes From Consumers Borrowing From the Future</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Barkin breaks down why spending stays strong despite high prices: consumers cut savings, fall behind on bills, and defer loan payments to keep spending; AI investment so far is more about price increases and crowding out than a productivity dividend; the Fed may face a long headwind.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Consumer resilience</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The US Treasury Secretary Threatens Allies with Divine Wrath, Proving He Has No Cards Treasury Secretary Bessent portrays America as an irritable god and demands allies submit like Pascal&#x27;s wager. Farrell sees through this op-ed: the louder the threat, the more it proves the US lacks the means to deliver, and allies have already begun to fend for themselves. Hidden Forces Hidden Forces Bessent&#x27;s Wager and Why the World is Calling America&#x27;s Bluff | Henry Farrell Trade War Dollar System Geopolitics US Institutions Financial Sanctions Pascal&#x27;s wager weaponized interdependence flexible realism unitary executive neo-royalism busted flush\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-hiddenforces-美国财长用神罚威胁盟国-反而证明手里没牌/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2b4203c8f6331570-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">51:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Hidden Forces</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>The US Treasury Secretary Threatens Allies with Divine Wrath, Proving He Has No Cards</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Treasury Secretary Bessent portrays America as an irritable god and demands allies submit like Pascal&#x27;s wager. Farrell sees through this op-ed: the louder the threat, the more it proves the US lacks the means to deliver, and allies have already begun to fend for themselves.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Trade War</span><span class=\"tag\">Dollar System</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Consciousness Has No Finish Line: The Brain Is Multiple Drafts, Not a Screening Room Dennett argues against the &quot;Cartesian Theater&quot;: there is no single finish line where all sensations pour into consciousness; the brain is parallel discriminations, multiple drafts that never get summed up, and even &quot;when did you become conscious of it&quot; often has no factual answer. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 399: Dan Dennett&#x27;s Model of Mind (Part One) Consciousness Philosophy Models of Mind Multiple Drafts Cognitive Science Cartesian Theater Multiple Drafts Model Phi phenomenon Qualia Homunculus Stalinesque vs. Orwellian\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-31-pel-笛卡尔剧场不存在-意识不是放映-而是多重草稿/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5a66360fe1170966-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0831</span></div>\n<h2>Consciousness Has No Finish Line: The Brain Is Multiple Drafts, Not a Screening Room</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dennett argues against the &quot;Cartesian Theater&quot;: there is no single finish line where all sensations pour into consciousness; the brain is parallel discriminations, multiple drafts that never get summed up, and even &quot;when did you become conscious of it&quot; often has no factual answer.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Consciousness</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span>\n</div></div></a>"]